<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Remonstrants</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Arminians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Remonstrants are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Protestant movement that followed the teaching of Jacobus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a>, who rejected strict Calvinist predestination. Named after their Remonstrance of 1610, they were condemned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> in 1618–1619, and their preachers were exiled. Some found refuge in Holstein-Gottorp, and the Remonstrant Brotherhood still exists in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jacobus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arminius">Arminius</a> disagreed with Calvin above all on predestination and defended free examination over the doctrines of established churches. In 1610 his followers presented the Five Articles of Remonstrance to the States of Holland and Friesland, setting out their disagreements with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a>. Their opponents, led by Franciscus Gomarus, became known as Gomarists or Counter-Remonstrants. Although the States General issued an edict tolerating both sides and forbidding further dispute, the quarrel merged with the political conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Maurice of Orange turned his political power against the Remonstrants. Their chief ally, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johan-van-oldenbarnevelt">Johan van Oldenbarnevelt</a>, was executed, other leaders were imprisoned, and city governments such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>'s were purged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dordrecht</a> expelled thirteen Arminian pastors led by Simon Episcopius, confirmed the Belgic Confession and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a>, and had Remonstrant ministers deposed and some banished. An exile community formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1619, and from 1621 exiles settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, where they built the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrichstadt">Friedrichstadt</a>.</p><p>Episcopius wrote their Confession in 1621, and Jan Uytenbogaert gave them a catechism and a church order. After Maurice's death in 1625 some exiles returned, and from 1630 they could live anywhere in the Republic, though until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> of 1795 they could not build churches and met in house churches. Their seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> trained Curcellaeus, Limborch, Wetstein and Le Clerc, and its theology grew more liberal and even rationalist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the mid-19th century the Remonstrant Brotherhood was shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> liberal theology. Its chief congregation is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>, and in 2016 it had about 5,000 members and friends in more than 40 congregations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, besides one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrichstadt">Friedrichstadt</a>. In 1986 it became the first Christian denomination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to allow the blessing of same-sex partnerships on an equal footing with marriage. Being non-creedal, it treats its confessions of 1621, 1940 and 2006 as without binding authority, and the wider Arminian current reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Remonstrants” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Holland paid over half of the common budget, and its chief legal officer, the Land’s Advocate, later called the Grand Pensionary, often directed policy, as Johan van Oldenbarnevelt did until his execution in 1619 after a clash with Maurice over the <b>Remonstrant</b> controversy in the Reformed Church.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the century the church split over predestination, and the Synod of Dort (1618–19) condemned the <b>Remonstrants</b>, who denied it, and banned their faith.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>He had clashed with Stadtholder Maurice of Nassau amid the conflict between <b>Remonstrants</b> and Counter-Remonstrants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes kept a splendid Lutheran court at Gottorp, where Duke Frederick III founded Friedrichstadt for Dutch <b>Remonstrants</b> and sponsored Adam Olearius's embassy to Persia and the great Gottorp globe.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Duchy of Holstein-Gottorp</a></li></ul></section>
